  "knowledgePattern": "Count the even prime exponents of square divisors",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use the unique prime factors",
      "body": "<p>Express the number as a product of prime powers. A divisor may use no more copies of each prime than the original number contains.</p>",
      "recap": "Divisibility is checked by comparing the exponents of each separate prime in a unique factorisation.",
      "pitfall": "Do not list only factors that happen to be obvious squares.",
      "pause": "Write the permitted exponent range for every prime.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-prime-factorisation",
          "reminder": "Divisibility is checked by comparing the exponents of each separate prime in a unique factorisation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Count the square-producing choices",
      "body": "<p>A square needs even prime exponents. Count the permitted choices independently, then apply the question’s lower restriction.</p>",
      "recap": "Different exponent choices give different positive divisors.",
      "pitfall": "The all-zero exponent choice has a special value and must be checked against the restriction.",
      "pause": "Calculate the count and remove any excluded divisor.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-prime-factorisation",
          "reminder": "Different exponent choices give different positive divisors."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>810000</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>81</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>10000</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Every positive divisor has form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><msup><mn>5</mn><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></math> with each exponent an integer from 0 to 4.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> A square has an even exponent of every prime, so each of a,b,c can be 0,2 or4. The independent exponent choices give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></math> square divisors.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> This includes 1, when all exponents vanish. The question requires integers greater than 1, leaving 26, answer B. Equivalently, each square divisor is the square of a divisor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>900</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>"
